      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A knocking sound from your electric water heater, and lukewarm water to boot - sounds like a bit of a problem. You might want to check the temperature and pressure relief valve, it's a common culprit when it comes to knocking noises. It's a bit like when a goblin's temper gets the better of him, and he starts banging away on his pots and pans - you need to find the source of the disturbance and address it.\n\nThat being said, if the valve is fine, it's possible that there's a blockage in the pipes or a faulty thermostat. You might need to call a plumber to diagnose the issue, but if it's just a simple fix, they might be able to get it up and running in no time. Just like how a goblin might be able to fix a leaky pipe with a bit of twine and some creative problem-solving.\n\nBefore you call the plumber, though, you might want to check the power to the water heater to make sure it's not just a tripped circuit. And if you're feeling handy, you could try checking the anode rod - it's a bit like a goblin's trusty sidekick, keeping the water heater running smoothly.\n\nBut if none",
